## What a `type: context` instance is

A first-class representation of a RUNTIME CONTEXT — the environment in which an actor operates.  Contexts capture facts like sandboxing, host class, firewall regime, and authorization scope.  Per the actor-context-rules deep-dive frame, runtime context is the UNIT to which firewall and authorization rules attach — distinct from actor identity (per identity-and-context orthogonality).

## When a context entity is warranted

- A distinguishable runtime environment exists (sandboxed work vs unsandboxed corpus authoring vs CI runner vs federated peer)
- Discipline rules differ across environments (the same actor behaves differently depending on context)
- Authorization scope varies (full corpus authority in one context; project-isolated firewall in another)

